// std/patterns.dag -- Reusable DAG shapes for the gunbc DSL.
//
// Patterns are compile-time expansion templates. Small patterns expand
// inline (no runtime SubDag boundary). Large patterns that represent
// meaningful user-facing workflows should be funcs instead.
//
// Architecture: patterns compose from fundamental steps. Each step is
// independently reusable (e.g., file_content_matches for staleness
// detection without a write). The composition patterns (upsert,
// content_upsert, transaction) add the wiring -- after/when edges --
// that gives the steps their operational meaning.
//
// See dsl-design.md 4.5 and Appendix H for the full pattern catalog.
//
// ── UPSERT<T> (operator canon 2026-05-29) ─────────────────────────────
// Operational rule: "do this" means upsert this — never blind create,
// never blind overwrite. The shape is fractal at every scale:
//
//   1. verify-first          — run Check (or lookup existing materialization)
//   2. satisfy-dependencies  — Create's inputs are themselves upserts (recursive)
//   3. create-if-missing     — Create runs only when should_act(check) (when edge)
//   4. cache-outcome         — Resolve returns the stable value (file path, decl id,
//                              memo slot) whether Check hit or Create ran
//
// Specializations (lightest → richest):
//   ensure<Check, Action>     — phases 1–3 on a Bool acted flag (no Resolve cache)
//   upsert<Check, Create, Resolve> — full four phases; Check/Create/Resolve are nodes
//   content_upsert            — upsert with file_content_matches + fs.write
//
// Effect algebra (`std/effects.dag`): UpsertEffect is the runtime witness for
// keyed convergent writes (lattice meet on Map<K,V>). Workflow patterns and
// UpsertEffect classify the same operational commitment at different layers.
//
// Compiler mirror (Rust bootstrap): same phases under other names —
// `find_equivalent_*` / `cardinality_idempotent_target` (verify-first),
// `ensure_optional_match_disj` (verify → create-if-missing → cache on Dag),
// `regen_bootstrap --verify` (verify-first for generated snapshots).
// Stray audit: docs/audit/upsert-pattern-compiler-stray-2026-05-29.md

module std.patterns

import std.resources { Filesystem }
import std.types { FilePath, Milliseconds }
import std.filesystem { partition_entries, is_text_readable }

// ── Upsert<T> type substrate skeleton (Phase 1.4a — v2-upsert-t-substrate-worksheet §1.1) ─
// Generic Upsert owns verify/create/resolve phase shape. Domain-specific dependency
// inputs belong on specializations such as CiUpsertStep<T> (Phase 1.5 — v2-ci-schema
// worksheet §1.2), not on generic Upsert<T>.
//
// 🟡 gated — feature:upsert-phase-substrate — staged: T is a payload brand until
// ResolveExpr<T> makes T load-bearing (E-P dedicated carriers).
// Phase bodies are typed nodes/expressions, not Symbol strings (P2).
// Dissolution: replace VerifyCheck/CreateAction/ResolveExpr Node aliases with dedicated
// phase carriers; P1.4-PARSER (A1) uncomments generic pattern bodies below.
//
// Phase 1.5 may reference Upsert<T> after this lands; active CI step migration must
// not rely on phase-body semantics until dedicated carriers or phase-class witnesses land.

// 🟡 gated — feature:upsert-phase-substrate — dissolve-on-arrival: dedicated carriers.
type VerifyCheck = Node
type CreateAction = Node
type ResolveExpr<T> = Node

type Upsert<T> {
  verify: VerifyCheck
  create: CreateAction
  resolve: ResolveExpr<T>
}

fn eq(a: String, b: String) -> Bool {
  a == b
}

